{
  "dom_id": "question-127241",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Write the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=4.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${P}_{3}(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(4)}{2!}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(4)}{3!}(x-4)^{3} \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(4)}{2}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(4)}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have the following set of values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4)=-5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4)=-7 \\\\ {f}^{″}(4)=-10 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(4)=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor Polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =-5-7(x-4)+\\frac{(-10)}{2}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(-13)}{6}(x-4)^{3} \\\\  & =-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
